// The parts of a hand-written parser that every grammar rewrites.
//
// A program that has to read a config format, a wire text format or a source language starts at the
// byte cursor and builds the same four things every time: a scanner, a span, literal scanning with
// its escape rules, and a way to say where the mistake was. This package is those four, plus the
// two that are fiddly enough to be worth writing once -- the layout pass an indentation-sensitive
// grammar needs, and the binding-power loop an expression grammar needs.
//
// **It is not a parser generator and not a combinator library.** A grammar stays hand-written
// recursive descent, which is what Clang, rustc, Go, TypeScript, Swift, Zig and javac all are; what
// this removes is the four hundred lines underneath it that are the same in all of them.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       parsing { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/parsing", version = "0.1.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `parsing`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.parsing`.
// The module carries the `sh.sysl.` prefix so that no package claims a top-level word another one
// might want, and the package name is the short one because it names this repository rather than a
// namespace.

package {
  name    = "parsing"
  version = "0.1.0"
}

// **`heap = true`, and the reason is one field.** `Source` holds a line-start table, and how many
// lines an input has is not known until it has been read, so the table is a `Buf[usize]`. Everything
// else here allocates nothing: `Scanner` is a slice and an offset, `Span` and `Loc` are two integers
// each, `literal` decodes into a buffer the caller supplies, and `pratt` is a loop over callbacks.
//
// **The alternative was refused deliberately.** The table could have been caller-supplied -- an
// array sized by counting newlines first -- which would have let the package state `requires {}` and
// run on a target with no allocator. What it costs is that every consumer counts newlines twice and
// carries storage whose size it has to have worked out, in order to reach a table that exists for
// the error path. The scanner tier is the part a microcontroller would want and it is the part that
// needs nothing; a program on such a target can use `Scanner` directly against its own bytes and
// never build a `Source`. That is a real cost of this clause and it is being accepted, not fixed:
// today the capability is claimed for the whole package, so such a program cannot depend on it at
// all.
//
// **What is NOT here is `os` or `posix`.** Nothing in this package opens, reads or writes anything.
// A `Source` is made over bytes the caller already has, and a diagnostic renders into a sink the
// caller passes -- so where the input came from and where the report goes are both the program's
// business.
requires {
  heap = true
}
